description:
Build a model of Driver Verifier's **special pool** (lesson 5.01) to see exactly how it turns silent memory bugs into immediate, explained failures. Each allocation gets its own page, placed so its last byte is right before an inaccessible guard page; the unused start of the page is filled with a pattern that is checked when the block is freed; freed pages become inaccessible.

```cpp
class SpecialPool
{
public:
    explicit SpecialPool(std::size_t pageSize);
    int allocate(std::size_t bytes, std::uint32_t tag);
    std::string write(int handle, std::size_t offset, std::size_t length);
    std::string read(int handle, std::size_t offset, std::size_t length) const;
    void underrun(int handle, std::size_t bytesBefore);
    std::string free(int handle, std::uint32_t tag);
    int pagesInUse() const;
    std::vector<std::string> unloadReport() const;
};
```

- **allocate** returns handles 1, 2, 3, ... for `1 <= bytes <= pageSize` (else 0, which is a failed allocation). The block occupies the **last** `bytes` bytes of a page of `pageSize` bytes; every byte before it is filled with `0xAA`.
- **write** and **read** describe an access to `length` bytes starting `offset` bytes into the block. In bounds: `""`. An access that goes past the block's end touches the guard page and faults at once: `"overrun by <n> bytes"` for a write, `"read past the end by <n> bytes"` for a read, where `n` is how far past the end the access reaches. An access to a freed block: `"write to freed memory"` / `"read of freed memory"`. An unknown handle: `"unknown handle"`. (A successful write stores nothing in this model; only the bounds matter.)
- **underrun** simulates a bug that writes `bytesBefore` bytes just before the block (into the fill area; at most the size of the fill area). It does not fault: special pool can only notice it later.
- **free**: `"unknown handle"`, `"double free"`, or `"tag mismatch"` (the block stays allocated). Otherwise the page is released and becomes inaccessible, and the result is `"ok"`, or `"underrun detected"` if the fill pattern before the block was changed.
- **unloadReport** lists the blocks never freed, grouped by tag, sorted by the tag's text (practice 3.01): `"Cirn: 2 allocations, 96 bytes"`; empty if nothing leaked.

